# Supplemental comparison review. These notes cover newly discovered leads;
# broad generic-title and other expressly pending questions are not waived.
SUPPLEMENTAL_REVIEW = {}
def supplement(numbers, basis):
    for n in numbers:
        assert n not in SUPPLEMENTAL_REVIEW
        SUPPLEMENTAL_REVIEW[n] = basis

supplement([13], 'Lambert Suavius Saint Paul is a sixteenth-century 19.8 x 12.1 cm engraving. The candidate is André Lambert’s 1933, 36.8 cm linocut in Les Indélicats. Different maker, physical technique, dimensions and dated composition.')
supplement([25,37,38,64,70,85], 'Numeric portfolio subnumbers are preserved. Search punctuation removal produces collisions with unrelated 2008 accessions: Degas oil paintings, Lucas van Leyden’s Gabriel drawing, Giani’s Three Fates, and Bramer’s Jael/Midas/Aeneas drawings. Read each returned object’s maker, title, technique and dimensions; none is the selected 1932–1936 linocut sheet. The Courtauld Degas citation contains another object’s inventory; that existing evidence is not changed or treated as proof for this candidate.')
supplement([121,122,127,135,140,141,148,150,151,160,168,182,185,186], 'Supplemental inventory matches are museum-local number collisions. Individually read titles, makers, materials, dimensions and national primary fields where the database is sparse. They depict different portraits, landscapes, still lifes, religious scenes or sculptures, with incompatible materials/scale and no candidate museum or native object identity. Bare numbers such as 10, 14, 15, 16, 26, 42 and 228 do not identify objects globally.')
supplement([123,124,126,191], 'The additional Bouchot Bonaparte comparator is Versailles MV 1952 / INV 2745, a 421 x 401 cm oil canvas from 1840. It is the painted composition, not one of the selected independently measured Frilley/Geoffroy paper impressions. Read the additional 27/271/273 accession collisions: unrelated named portraits, landscape drawings, a Puget altar design, and Rembrandt’s Shepherds etching. None supplies the same physical impression.')
supplement([129,130,145,146,147,155,173,175,176,179,180,192,194], 'Individually read supplemental Saint-Cloud place-name matches and full primary fields. Existing Ben, Printemps, Fillette, Roqueplan, Fichel, Morière, Dantan, Langlacé, Brune and Sarazin de Belmont objects are oil paintings of other scenes and sizes; Bouchon’s fête is a 54 x 68 cm gouache from 1761. They are not the selected named-printmaker impressions or Monnet wash drawing. Additional Empress portraits and illustrated imperial receptions have different compositions, dates, physical media and measurements. Museum-local numeric accession collisions are unrelated works. The related G988.18.50 print did not produce an existing object match; it is not merged with Monnet’s preparatory wash.')
supplement([132], 'The sparse Avelines Marguerite record resolves to M0416000008 / 69.1.5, an oil canvas 46.2 x 38.3 cm, not this 1901 lithograph 69.1.52. Orsay and Strasbourg 1898 portraits are also paintings. Strasbourg XXXII 81 is a separately acquired 1932 lithograph numbered 10/10; the Avelines print is from Charles Oulmont’s 19 November 1969 donation. Chicago 1927.1629 has a 56.7 x 49.2 cm sheet; Cleveland 1941.195 has a 27.3 x 21.8 cm image. The candidate’s separate signed, framed 42.7 x 34.2 cm museum object and provenance support an independent impression. Carrière self-portrait/palette/Jean René titles are different subjects; no source alias match.')
supplement([133], 'The sparse unverified Madame Eugène Carrière entry has supplied Orsay provenance. The frozen full national metadata supplies five exact-title Orsay objects, RF 3117/3135/3118/3108/3130, all oil paintings; no guessed choice among these is made. The candidate is a signed, framed 1893 lithograph from Oulmont’s 1969 donation. Chicago 1921.365 is the smaller 18.2 x 12.5 cm image. Cleveland 1941.196 and NGA 2001.136.48 retain separate institution accessioned sheets; Strasbourg 55.002.0.45 is another lithographic impression with the same plate size. Plate equality does not merge those physical holdings; retain candidate’s specific donation and frame context. Remaining shared plate/source provenance checks are explicit, not hidden by the title comparison.')
supplement([144], 'The sparse same-maker study is M0416005578 / 2021.3.3, an 89 x 116 cm oil canvas. The candidate is a 17 x 23 cm pen/ink drawing from 1895. Exact 2014.1 accession collisions are a Cleveland wooden mask, a Jordaens biblical drawing, a Levigne oil and a Sargent stable painting. Different material, composition and dimensions resolve all displayed leads.')
supplement([149], 'The additional Amour titles are Ingres drawings of separate heads, limbs, wings and a lyre, with graphite/chalk and small measured sheets, not the Dubufe/Maile/Reynolds aquatint of a seated woman holding a letter. Inventory 2018.2.1 collisions are Motley and Bernard oils, a Millet ceiling design and a Claudet terracotta portrait. No physical identity evidence.')
supplement([158,159], 'The two Garbizza/Chapuis impressions share old 303 but current suffixes, sheet widths 30 and 32 cm, and watercolor treatment establish different sheets. Existing 303 matches are unrelated Preudhomme/Regnault/Bréauté paintings, Laurens/Watteau drawings, Belin Triboulet, a still life and another named sitter. Saint-Cloud oil views by Fillette and Langlacé differ in technique and composition. No object is separated on inventory alone.')
supplement([163,189], 'Read every additional Saint-Cloud comparator, including sparse entries using full national records. They are landscape/river oil paintings, named nineteenth-century royal receptions and newspaper illustrations, a Prins evening landscape, or a Pigache portrait. None depicts Geo Ham’s 1946 automobile race in pastel. None is the small Wyld-workshop fête gouache; the comparable Bouchon gouache is dated 1761 and measures 54 x 68 cm, while the candidate’s entire frame is 31.2 x 38.7 cm. Other 607 accessions are Foujita’s nude, Heinlein’s Meran landscape and Doré’s large oil.')
supplement([172], 'Supplemental Saint-Cloud landscapes and fêtes have incompatible scenes/materials/dimensions. The 1874 newspaper record G988.19.38 depicts the legislature in 1852, not the candidate’s Brumaire session. Four Ingres Saint drawings are small graphite figures; Bernard’s park perspective is a 1935 red wash. Old 414 collisions include Chavannes and Gayfejian landscapes and a seventeenth-century oil-on-copper church interior. No same physical print.')
supplement([187], 'New inventory punctuation collisions are Franceschini’s 29.9 x 21.8 cm chalk drawing and Lot and his Daughters oil DPG629 (106.3 x 89.7 cm), not the attributed Tocqué armored male portrait, 58 x 46.5 cm.')
supplement([188], 'The newly found La Touche portrait of Cirasse is an 80 x 98 cm oil canvas from 1907, not Tournoux’s 50 cm plaster bust. The 2008.0.2 collision is a 61.2 x 50.1 cm Breitel oil portrait.')
supplement([196], 'NGA 2002.157.6 is explicitly credited to Ruth Cole Kainen, with a 31.7 x 24.6 cm chine-collé sheet, in the retained official metadata citations. Avelines 69.1.53 comes from Charles Oulmont’s 1969 donation and is separately signed in its lower margin and framed. Same printed composition/near-equal plate does not collapse these independently documented impressions.')
